Z||000000||Philphine||05-27-2001||10:01 AM||||<BR>the latest hood vent thread got me thinking, if you couldn't carry a spare in the front (or the back in my car), what kind of emergency stuff would you carry. a lot of fix a flat i'm sure. maybe one of those tubeless tire repair kits with the sticky strips that you poke into the hole with rubber glue. a portable air pump and the jack in case i have to take the tire off and take it to be repaired. and i guess a cell phone.<P>anything else? <P> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Black88GT||05-27-2001||04:30 PM||||I just keep mine under my porch and keep a charged cell phone at all times.  Usually someone in my large family is home that I can bribe them to bring the spare for me if need be (or call them on their cell phones).  However I don't do much long distance driving, maybe 30 min. away at the most.  If I did a lot of long distance driving I would carry one for sure because there's no way they would deliver my spare if I was in another state!<P>Anyone know if those cans of "Fix-a-Flat" really work?  I always wondered about them.<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000002||Archie||05-27-2001||04:40 PM||||I carry a Cell Phone and an AutoClub Card.<P>Archie||||reg||1
Z||000003||Nashco||05-27-2001||04:48 PM||||Triple A (AAA)...60 bucks a year and free towing within five miles.  Another buck a mile after that when I had to get my other car towed before.  Oh, and if you don't have a cell phone, get with it!  It's the 21st century, and they don't cost any more than a regular phone nowadays.<P>Also, FIX A FLAT SUCKS.  I worked at a tire store for about 7 months before.  I never saw/heard of anybody who had used it and made the leak go away.  Some people went as far to think, "Hmm...one can didn't work.  I'll try another, that should do the trick."  Do you realize what 25 ounces of liquid in your tire does to ride quality?  I can't believe that people think a liquid inside the tire won't stay a liquid outside the tire; it's all air on both sides, not any special air inside the tire!  In summation...no it doesn't work, and it's god awful to clean out of the tire after the leak *doesn't* go away (horrible ammonia smell).  Please never use fix-a-flat, do yourself, your tire, and your local tire shop a favor.<P>Oh, and I'm sure there's people out there that it has worked for...but it's definitely not something you want to rely on, the tow company is a better bet <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ed||05-27-2001||05:33 PM||||Some Ferrari models came with no spare, but a can of sealant.<P>On other Ferraris, the spare only fits the back, but not the front (too large). ||||reg||1
Z||000005||pontiacs4me||05-27-2001||10:33 PM||||I feel a subwoofer in the front is more important than a spare.  I carry a plug kit, one with the sticky rubber strips and glue, and a can of fix a flat, one thing about fix a flat, If you remove the object that caused the leak, the fix a flat most likely wont work.  Say for example you have a nail in your tire.  Leave the nail in the tire, put in some fix a flat, it will begin to leak out around the nail and it dries between the nail and the tire.  It creates a seal.  someone mentioned how air being on both sides of the tire, yeah blah blah,  the fix a flat dries and creates the seal at the puncture.  And say you are in BFE at 2 am, would you rather ruin a tire and the guy at the tire stores day, or walk....||||reg||1
